{
  "dom_id": "step-q22809",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x) \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
